Run the matching off the boot thread again

The matching has run on the boot thread since run 544, and every
round since has stopped inside it.  A driver probe parks (the panel
ends at P1 with no nio: P), the boot thread is the one running the
matching, so bsd_init and the RAMDisk root are never reached, and
the board resets before the userland.

Run 543 is the control: the matching worked there, the platform
expert started, three drivers were probed, and the boot thread got
past the matching to w8m3, where it waited for the CPU count.  That
wait is already fixed: the count is published directly now.

What is left is only that the matching must not hold the boot thread.
The userland this board boots is the mockfs image in RAM -- rd=md0,
the N4KROOT rootfs with the real launchd -- not a volume published by
a matched storage driver.  Nothing after the matching needs a driver
to have matched.  So the matching goes back onto a thread of its own,
as the old note in this very file describes.  If a probe never comes
back it stalls that thread alone; the boot thread goes on to bsd_init,
vfs_mountroot and launchd.  The mark "MT thread" says the thread was
created, "MT done" says the matching returned, and "MT inline" is the
fallback if the thread could not be started.

diff --git a/osfmk/kern/startup.c b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
index 5c4ff34..8fecfd2 100644
--- a/osfmk/kern/startup.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/startup.c
@@ -626,6 +626,27 @@ nio_heartbeat_thread(void *arg, wait_result_t wr)
 	}
 }
 
+/* nio: the matching runs on a thread of its own again.
+ *
+ * The userland this board boots is the mockfs RAMDisk, not a volume
+ * published by a matched storage driver.  What the boot thread must
+ * never do is wait behind a driver whose start() does not come back:/n * run 543 showed the matching itself works, and every round since has
+ * died because the matching ran on the boot thread and one probe
+ * parked there, so bsd_init and the RAMDisk root were never reached.
+ * On its own thread the matching is free to finish or to stall; either
+ * way the boot thread goes on to bsd_init and the userland.
+ */
+static void
+nio_matching_thread(void *arg, wait_result_t wr)
+{
+	(void) arg;
+	(void) wr;
+	nio_match_waypoint = 1;
+	PE_lockdown_iokit();
+	nio_match_waypoint = 2;
+	nio_dc_puts("\nnio: MT done");
+}
+
 /* nio: the matching runs on its own thread.
  *
  * Run 546 and run 548 both stopped between the S1 mark and the S2 mark, and
@@ -1071,25 +1092,15 @@ kernel_bootstrap_thread(void)
 #ifdef  IOKIT
 	kernel_bootstrap_log("PE_lockdown_iokit");
 	{
-		/* nio: the matching runs on this thread, the way it ran on
-		 * 2026-11-19 before the round that moved it to its own.
-		 *
-		 * The photograph of run 543 (2026-11-19 15:20) is what the
-		 * board looked like when the matching worked: the platform
-		 * expert started, and ArmCpu, NioUfs and AppleARMCPU were
-		 * probed.  On its own thread the matching ran at the same
-		 * time as the rest of the boot, and it met somebody who was
-		 * holding a registry lock: the waypoint said 40, which is the
-		 * name comparison, and the thread never came back.
-		 *
-		 * On this thread it is the same order as the board's own:
-		 * publish the personalities, then match, then carry on.
-		 */
-		nio_dc_puts("\nnio: MT inline");
-		nio_match_waypoint = 1;
-		PE_lockdown_iokit();
-		nio_match_waypoint = 2;
-		nio_dc_puts("\nnio: MT done");
+		thread_t nio_mt = THREAD_NULL;
+		if (kernel_thread_start(nio_matching_thread, NULL, &nio_mt)
+		    == KERN_SUCCESS) {
+			thread_deallocate(nio_mt);
+			nio_dc_puts("\nnio: MT thread");
+		} else {
+			nio_dc_puts("\nnio: MT inline");
+			PE_lockdown_iokit();
+		}
 	}
 	printf("\nR4 iokit lockdown");
 	nio_boot_waypoint = 7;
